:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-differentiation-stepanov/</guid><description>Three approaches to computing derivatives, forward-mode AD, reverse-mode AD, and finite differences, each with different trade-offs for numerical computing and machine learning.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-differentiation-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Calculus</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>automatic-differentiation</category><category>numerical-methods</category><category>calculus</category><category>machine-learning</category><category>dual-numbers</category></item><item><title>How to Teach a Computer Calculus with Pattern Matching</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-10-term-rewriting/</link><pubDate>Sat, 15 Oct 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-10-term-rewriting/</guid><description>Define patterns, define replacements, repeat until done. Watch a 90-line rewrite engine learn to differentiate.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-10-term-rewriting/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Calculus</media:title></media:content><category>term-rewriting</category><category>pattern-matching</category><category>symbolic-computation</category><category>python</category><category>interactive</category><category>calculus</category></item><item><title>Numerical Differentiation</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-04-finite-diff-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Tue, 12 Apr 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-04-finite-diff-stepanov/</guid><description>Choosing step size h for finite differences: small enough for a good approximation, not so small that floating-point errors eat your lunch.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-04-finite-diff-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Calculus</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>numerical-methods</category><category>differentiation</category><category>error-analysis</category><category>calculus</category></item><item><title>Forward-Mode Automatic Differentiation</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2021-09-dual-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Mon, 20 Sep 2021 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2021-09-dual-stepanov/</guid><description>Dual numbers extend our number system with an infinitesimal epsilon where epsilon^2 = 0. Evaluating f(x + epsilon) yields f(x) + epsilon * f&amp;#39;(x)—the derivative emerges automatically from the algebra.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2021-09-dual-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Calculus</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>automatic-differentiation</category><category>dual-numbers</category><category>calculus</category><category>machine-learning</category></item></channel></rss>